#{[17592186093402 "If the $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ is invertible, then we can find a solution to the system \n\n$$A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$$\n\nby \"pre-multiplying\" both sides of the equation by $A^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}A\\mathbf{x} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {A}^{-1}A\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {I}_{2}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, to find the solution of $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ we simply calculate $\\mathbf{x}=A^{-1}\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nFor instance, to find the solution to the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n\t=\n\t\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n we first calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\nA^{-1} =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen, the solution to this system is\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo the solution is $x=3$ and $y=2.$"] [17592186116491 "Consider a system of two linear equations with two variables \n\n$$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\color{red}{a}_{11}}x+{\\color{red}{a}_{12}}y &={\\color{blue}{b}_{1}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{a}_{21}}x+{\\color{red}{a}_{22}}y &={\\color{blue}{b}_{2}},\\end{aligned}\\right.$$\n\nand let\n$$A = \n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}{a}_{11}} & {\\color{red}{a}_{12}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{a}_{21}} & {\\color{red}{a}_{22}}\\end{bmatrix},\n\\quad\n\\mathbf{x} = \n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\n\\quad\n\\mathbf{b} =\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}{b}_{1}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{b}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.$$\n\nRecall that we can write the system as $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}.$ The **invertible matrix theorem** says the following statements are equivalent:\n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible (that is, $A^{-1}$ exists).\n\n2\\. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}$ (we call such systems consistent independent).\n\n3\\. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique solution $$\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$$\n\n4\\. The lines ${\\color{red}{a_{11}}}x+{\\color{red}{a_{12}}}y={\\color{blue}{b_1}}$ and ${\\color{red}{a_{21}}}x+{\\color{red}{a_{22}}}y={\\color{blue}{b_2}}$ intersect at one point.\n\n5\\. The  determinant of $A$ is nonzero (that is, $\\det(A) \\neq 0$)."] [17592186116498 "Suppose we want to find the inverse of the matrix \n\n$$\nA = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$ \n\nWe can compute the inverse of $\\color{blue}A$ using elementary row operations. There are two key advantages to using row operations to compute the inverse of a matrix:\n\n- The method works for any invertible matrix of any size, not just $2\\times 2$ matrices.\n\n- It requires significantly less computation than some alternative methods. This is especially true for large matrices.\n\nHere, we'll focus on the $2\\times 2$ case. We use the following fact:\n\n*The row operations that transform $\\color{blue}A$ into the identity matrix $I_2$ also transform $I_2$ into $A^{-1}$*.\n\nSo, to find the inverse of $A,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1:** Write down $\\color{blue}A$ and the identity matrix $I_2$ side-by-side, as shown below:\n$$[{\\color{blue}{A}}\\, |\\, {\\color{black}I_2}]\t\n=\t\n\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}{\\color{blue}1} & {\\color{blue}2} & 1 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{blue}3} & {\\color{blue}7} & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right]$$\n\n**Step 2:** Apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $\\color{blue}A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{}[{\\color{blue}A\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}{I}_{2}\\hspace{0.167em}]} & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}{\\color{blue}1} & {\\color{blue}2} & 1 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{blue}3} & {\\color{blue}7} & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & {\\color{red}7} & {\\color{red}-2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & {\\color{red}-3} & {\\color{red}1}\\end{array}\\right] \\\\  & =[\\hspace{0.167em}I\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{red}{A}^{-1}}]\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\nA^{-1} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}7 & - 2 \\\\ - 3 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nWe can easily check our result by computing $AA^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}A{A}^{-1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}$$"] [17592186121018 "Sometimes there are lots of calculations involved in finding the determinant of a matrix using a cofactor expansion across the first row. \n\nFortunately, however, we don't always have to use the first row! We can use *any* row we like. A cofactor expansion across any general row is known as a **Laplace expansion**.\n\nAcross the row $i,$ the Laplace expansion of an $n\\!\\times\\! n$ matrix $A$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\det(A) & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{\\color{red}{a}_{ij}}{\\color{black}{C}_{ij}} \\\\  & ={\\color{red}{a}_{i1}}{\\color{black}{C}_{i1}}+{\\color{red}{a}_{i2}}{\\color{black}{C}_{i2}}+⋯+{\\color{red}{a}_{in}}{\\color{black}{C}_{in}},\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{blue}C_{ij}}$ is the cofactor of the entry $\\color{red}a_{ij}$ in $A.$ Remember the cofactor of the entry $\\color{red}a_{ij}$ is \n\n$${\\color{black}C_{ij}}=(-1)^{i+j}{\\color{black}M_{ij}},$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{black}M_{ij}}$ is the minor of the entry.\n\nFor example, consider the following matrix:\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$B=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nTo compute the determinant of the matrix above, it is most convenient to use a Laplace expansion along the row with the most zeros. So, let's use Laplace expansion across the third row ($i=3$) as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\det \\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}-1 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}2} & {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}0} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3\\end{array} \\right] & \\displaystyle  = \\sum _{j = 1}^{n} {\\color{red}{a}_{3 j}} {\\color{black}{C}_{3 j}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\color{red}0} \\cdot  {\\color{black}{C}_{31}} + {\\color{red}2} \\cdot  {\\color{blue}{C}_{32}} + {\\color{red}0} \\cdot  {\\color{black}{C}_{33}} + {\\color{red}0} \\cdot  {\\color{black}{C}_{34}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\color{red}2} \\cdot  {\\color{blue}( - 1 {)}^{3 + 2}} \\cdot  {\\color{blue}\\enclose{left right}{\\begin{array}{ccc}\\displaystyle - 1 & \\displaystyle 1 & \\displaystyle 1 \\\\ \\displaystyle 0 & \\displaystyle 1 & \\displaystyle 1 \\\\ \\displaystyle 0 & \\displaystyle 0 & \\displaystyle - 3\\end{array}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = - 2 \\cdot  \\enclose{left right}{\\begin{array}{ccc}\\displaystyle - 1 & \\displaystyle 1 & \\displaystyle 1 \\\\ \\displaystyle 0 & \\displaystyle 1 & \\displaystyle 1 \\\\ \\displaystyle 0 & \\displaystyle 0 & \\displaystyle - 3\\end{array}}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nSo, we reduced a $4 \\times 4$ determinant to a $3 \\times 3$ determinant."] [17592186121019 "We can also use the Laplace expansion across any *column* to find the determinant. Across the column $j,$ the Laplace expansion is given by the formula\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\det(A) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{\\color{red}{a}_{ij}}{\\color{blue}{C}_{ij}} \\\\  & ={\\color{red}{a}_{1j}}{\\color{blue}{C}_{1j}}+{\\color{red}{a}_{2j}}{\\color{blue}{C}_{2j}}+⋯+{\\color{red}{a}_{nj}}{\\color{blue}{C}_{nj}},\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $\\color{blue}C_{ij}$ is the cofactor of the entry ${\\color{red}a_{ij}}.$\n\nIn other words, the Laplace expansion across a row or column is the sum of the entries in the row or column multiplied by their respective cofactors.\n\nTo find the determinant of a matrix, we can choose to use the Laplace expansion across any row or column, so choose the row or column that has the most zeros, to cut down on the calculations!"] [17592186121049 "Suppose we know that a plane $\\Pi$ passes through the point $P(1,1,2)$ and that the vector $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 0,0,3 \\rangle$ is perpendicular to the plane. Let's write down an equation of this plane using the dot product.\n\n![](images/0f/0f8353480099369ad92701d1799f72d46eab4bbe5c17add8cb37a4f34074311b.png)\n\nWe start by considering an arbitrary point $R(x,y,z)$ that lies on the plane. The position vectors of the points $R$ and $P$ are $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 1,1,2 \\rangle$ respectively. So, the vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{r}-⟨1,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis parallel to the plane. Therefore, $\\overrightarrow{PR} \\perp \\mathbf{n},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/28/28b6770bd681257b1030679a421b80e3f3d1e8fbb86d2060b64c7301d0878c5c.png)\n\nNow, recall that if two nonzero vectors are perpendicular, then their dot product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{PR}\\perp \\mathbf{n} \\quad \\Leftrightarrow \\quad \\overrightarrow{PR}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} = 0\n$$\n\nSo, we get the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is called the **vector equation of the plane** that passes through the point $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}.$ In our case, we obtain\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r} - \\langle 1,1,2 \\rangle) \\cdot \\langle 0,0,3 \\rangle = 0.\n$$\n\n**Note:** Using the properties of the dot product, we can arrive at an alternative vector equation given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c = \\mathbf{p} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}$ is a (scalar) constant."] [17592186121050 "If we are given an equation of a plane using the dot product, we can always obtain an equation in terms of $x,$ $y,$ and $z.$ For example, suppose we have the plane that passes through the point $P(-7,5,-2)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 3,-6,5 \\rangle.$\n\nFirst, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -7,5,-2 \\rangle,$ and we can write down the equation of the plane using the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨3,-6,5⟩ & =⟨-7,5,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-6,5⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨3,-6,5⟩ & =-61\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-6,5⟩ & =-61 \\\\ 3x-6y+5z & =-61 \\\\ 3x-6y+5z+61 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis final equation is called the **Cartesian equation of the plane**.\n\n![](images/77/77f18981dda500889655225eabbec730b7b295e6b16cec1700d56c2e27e0ac91.png)\n\nNote that in the equation \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}x+({\\color{blue}-6})y+{\\color{blue}5}z+61 = 0,\n$$\n\nthe coefficients next to $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ are the coordinates of the normal vector \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle {\\color{blue}3},{\\color{blue}-6},{\\color{blue}5} \\rangle\n$$\n\n."] [17592186121062 "To add two vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ we just need to add their corresponding components. To illustrate, consider the vectors $\\mathbf{a},\\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{R}^4,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ - 3 \\\\ 6\\end{array} \\right]}, \\quad \\quad \\mathbf{b}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nTo compute $\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b},$ all we have to do is to add each component of the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ with the corresponding component of $\\mathbf{b}.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}}+{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}5} \\\\ {\\color{red}1} \\\\ {\\color{red}-3} \\\\ {\\color{red}6}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}-5} \\\\ {\\color{blue}3} \\\\ {\\color{blue}1} \\\\ {\\color{blue}0}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}5}+({\\color{blue}-5}) \\\\ {\\color{red}1}+{\\color{blue}3} \\\\ {\\color{red}-3}+{\\color{blue}1} \\\\ {\\color{red}6}+{\\color{blue}0}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can only compute the sum, difference, or dot product of two vectors if both vectors have the same number of components. For example, we can't find the following sum, so we say it's *undefined*:\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]} +  {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 5\\end{array} \\right]} = {\\color{red}\\times} \\:(\\textrm{undefined})\n$$"] [17592186121063 "Let's again consider the following matrix:\n\n$$\nA={\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nTo find an expression for the null space using set-builder notation, we need to find all the solutions of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$ Let's start by considering the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\, \\mathbf{0}]$ and finding its reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[A\\,|\\,0] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st column). The second column is not a pivot column.\n\nNow, we convert the reduced matrix back into a system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\color{blue}{x}_{1}}+3{\\color{red}{x}_{2}} &=0 \\\\ 0 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, $\\color{blue}x_1$ is a basic variable (it corresponds to the pivot column), while $\\color{red}x_2$ is a free variable (it corresponds to the non-pivot column). Because there is a free variable, the system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has infinitely many solutions. \n\nFrom the first equation we get ${\\color{blue}x_1}=-3{\\color{red}x_2},$ so the set of all the solutions of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ is\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A) = \n\t\\left\\{  {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 3 {\\color{red}{x}_{2}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{x}_{2}}\\end{array} \\right]} \\: : \\: {\\color{red}x_2} \\in\\mathbb{R} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It is not necessary to consider the full augmented matrix $[A \\,|\\, \\mathbf{0}]$ since the last column won't be changed during the process of reduction. Really, we just need to find the reduced row echelon form of the matrix $A$ and then set up the corresponding system."] [17592186121120 "Suppose we have a plane with the following normal vector: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle -1,2,1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nLet's use the scalar product to find a vector that is parallel to this plane.\n\nWe denote our parallel vector as $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle t_1,t_2,t_3 \\rangle.$ Now, since $\\mathbf{v} \\perp \\mathbf{n},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{v}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,2,1⟩ & =0 \\\\ -{t}_{1}+2{t}_{2}+{t}_{3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to find a nonzero solution to this equation. To do that, we solve the equation for *one* of the variables (it doesn't matter which one). For example, let's solve our equation for the variable $t_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =2{t}_{2}+{t}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the variable $t_1$ depends on $t_2$ and $t_3,$ which, in turn, can be *any* real numbers. The variables $t_2$ and $t_3$ are called **free variables**.\n\nHence, the general form of our vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=\\langle 2t_2+t_3, \\, t_2, \\, t_3 \\rangle,\n$$\n\nwhere $t_2, t_3 \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nTo find a particular vector parallel to the plane, we can substitute any values for $t_2$ and $t_3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- For example, setting $t_2=0$ and $t_3=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_1= \\langle 1, 0, 1 \\rangle.\n$$\n\n- Or, we could set $t_2=1$ and $t_3=0$ and get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\langle 2, 1, 0 \\rangle.\n$$\n\n**Note:** There are infinitely many vectors parallel to a given plane. In our example, we found only two particular vectors."] [17592186125785 "We can find the eigenvalues of a $3\\times 3$ matrix in much the same way as for $2\\!\\times\\! 2$ matrices. \n\nRemember, $\\lambda$ is an eigenvalue of $A$ if $A\\mathbf{v} = \\lambda\\mathbf{v}$ for some non-zero vector $\\mathbf{v}$. This implies that the equation\n\n$$(A-\\lambda I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$$\n\nhas a non-trivial solution, which means that the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, and consequently\n$\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0.$\nTherefore, if we want to find the eigenvalues of a $n\\!\\times\\! n$ matrix $A,$ we have to find the solutions of the characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0.\n$$\n\nLet's go through this process in the case of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}- 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & - 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix $A-\\lambda I{:}$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\lambda \\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set and solve the characteristic equation $\\,\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ Using the fact that the determinant of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix is given by the product of the elements on the main diagonal, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )(-8-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +8)(\\lambda -5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are $\\lambda=-2,$ $\\lambda=-8,$ and $\\lambda=5.$"] [17592186125786 "The **algebraic multiplicity** of an eigenvalue $\\lambda$ is the number of times it is a solution of the characteristic equation. \n\nFor example, consider a $6\\times 6$ matrix $A$ with the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$(\\lambda-{\\color{red}4})(\\lambda-{\\color{red}4})(\\lambda-1)(\\lambda+8)^{\\color{blue}3} =0$$\n\nThe eigenvalue $\\lambda={\\color{red}4}$ is a double root. Therefore, it has algebraic multiplicity $2.$ \n\nBy the same reasoning, we say that \n\n- $\\lambda=1$ has algebraic multiplicity $1,$ and\n\n- $\\lambda=-8$ has algebraic multiplicity $\\color{blue}3$."] [17592186135045 "A set of vectors ${U}=\\{ \\mathbf{u}_1, \\cdots, \\mathbf{u}_p \\}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is an **orthogonal set** if each pair of distinct vectors from the set is orthogonal.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following set:\n\n$$\n{W}=\\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ - 4 \\\\ 7\\end{array} \\right]} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nIf we denote the vectors in our set ${W}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{w}_1, \\mathbf{w}_2, \\mathbf{w}_3 \\},$ we can check that ${W}$ is orthogonal by showing that \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{w}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {\\mathbf{w}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {\\mathbf{w}}_{2}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's compute each dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{w}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=3\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1=0 & {\\color{green}✓} \\\\ {\\mathbf{w}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}=3\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 7=0 & {\\color{green}✓} \\\\ {\\mathbf{w}}_{2}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}=(-1)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 7=0 & {\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo indeed, ${W}$ is an orthogonal set.\n\nIf an orthogonal set is also a basis, then we call it an **orthogonal basis**. Here, $W$ is an orthogonal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ because it contains three linearly independent (you can check this) orthogonal vectors."] [17592186135090 "A set of vectors ${U}=\\{\\mathbf{u}_1, \\cdots, \\mathbf{u}_p \\}$ is an **orthonormal set** if it is an orthogonal set of *unit* vectors. Remember that $\\mathbf u_i$ is a unit vector if $\\Vert \\mathbf u_i \\Vert = 1,$ or equivalently, $\\mathbf u_i \\cdot \\mathbf u_i = 1.$\n\nAn orthonormal set that is also a basis, like the standard basis, is called an **orthonormal basis**. For instance, the standard basis $\\{\\mathbf{e}_1, \\mathbf{e}_2, \\mathbf{e}_3\\}$ of $\\mathbb R^3$ is an orthonormal basis because the vectors are orthogonal to each other and each vector in the set is a unit vector.\n\nThere are two more things to note about orthogonal sets:\n\n- Any orthogonal set of non-zero vectors is linearly independent.\n\n- An orthogonal set of non-zero vectors in $\\Bbb R^n$ cannot contain more than $n$ vectors. More precisely, if $V = \\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\dots, \\mathbf{v}_p\\}$ is an orthogonal set of non-zero vectors and $\\mathbf{v}_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^n,$ then we must have that $p\\leq n.$\n\n**Note:** To remember that an orthogonal set of non-zero vectors in $\\Bbb R^n$ cannot contain more than $n$ vectors, you can use the following intuitive mnemonic. There are only $n$ dimensions in $\\Bbb R^n,$ and each non-zero orthogonal vector has a $1$-dimensional span, meaning that it \"takes up\" $1$ dimension of $\\Bbb R^n.$ So, you can't \"fit\" more than $n$ orthogonal vectors in $\\Bbb R^n.$"] [17592186153353 "In the previous example, we computed a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A),$ where \n\n$$\nA={\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 2 & - 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 5\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nThe basis that we found previously was\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B} = \\Bigg\\{ \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}_1}, \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}_2} \n\t\\Bigg\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Col}(A) = \\textrm{Span}\\,\\mathcal B\n= \\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf a_1, \\mathbf a_2\\}\n\n= \n\\textrm{Span} \\Bigg\\{ \n\t\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]}, \n\t\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t\\Bigg\\}.\n$$\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{a}_3$ was not included in the basis because the $3$rd column of $A$ was not a pivot column. This means that $\\mathbf{a}_3$ can be written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$\n\nNow, what if we want to find the exact linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ that yields $\\mathbf{a}_3?$ That is to say, we want to find $x_1, x_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that \n\n$$\n{\\color{black}x_1} \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_1} + {\\color{black}x_2} \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_2} = \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 5\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_3}.\n$$\n\nThis is equivalent to a system with the augmented matrix\n$$\n\\left[\n\t{\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & - 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 5\\end{array}}\n\t\\right].\n$$\n\nIf we reduced this matrix to the *reduced* row echelon form, we get\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t{\\begin{array}{cc|c}\\bbox[3pt,lightgray]{ {\\color{blue}1}} & 0 & {\\color{red}3} \\\\ 0 & \\bbox[3pt,lightgray]{ {\\color{blue}1}} & {\\color{red}- 2}\\end{array}}\n\t\\right].\n$$\n\nFinally, the third column of the *reduced* matrix tells us that $x_1=3$ and $x_2=-2,$ meaning that\n$$\n\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 5\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_3} = {\\color{red}3}\\cdot \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_1} + ({\\color{red}-2})\\cdot \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_2}.\n$$\n\nIn general, if the $n$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express $\\mathbf{a}_n$ as a linear combination of the pivot columns of the original matrix. \n\n**Watch out!** The matrix must be written in *reduced* row echelon form (with $1$'s for pivots and $0$'s above and below the pivots), not just in a row echelon form."] [17592186193876 "Recall that a nonempty subset $H\\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ is a **subspace** of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n- *Closure under vector addition:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n- *Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nIn the vector space $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ there are two **trivial subspaces**.\n\n- One trivial subspace is the set \n\n$$\nH=\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}\n$$\n\n consisting of just the zero vector. This is a subspace because the sum of two zero vectors is the zero vector, and any multiple of the zero vector is the zero vector.\n\n- Another trivial subspace is the set \n\n$$\nH = \\mathbb{R}^n\n$$\n\n consisting of the entire set $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ This is a subspace because the sum of two vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ and any multiple of a vector in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is in $\\mathbb{R}^n.$"] [17592186199551 "We can use set-builder notation to represent sets that lie in the coordinate plane.\n\nFor example, let's describe the following shaded region using set-builder-notation.\n\n![](images/2c/2c4545ab59c1bf6847ab38b09ddf8b86c3227bbc6006835aceb8637b45bff817.png)\n\n  \n\nFirst, notice that a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the rectangle if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2<x<6,\\hspace{2em}2<y<5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that we also need to   \n\n- *include* the left edge ($x=2$), and\n\n- *include* the right edge ($x=6$).\n\nTherefore, we change the corresponding inequality symbols to non-strict, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}x\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}6,\\hspace{2em}2<y<5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since *every* real value in these intervals is included, $x\\in \\mathbb R, y\\in\\mathbb R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R = \\mathbb R^2.\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{\n(x,y) \\in \\mathbb R^2\n\\: : \\:  \n\n2 \n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq} \nx \n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq}  \n6, \n\\: \n2 \n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\lt} \ny\n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\lt}   \n5 \n\\Big\\}.\n$$"] [17592186205256 "Recall that the cross product of two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b} = | \\, \\mathbf{a} \\, | \\cdot | \\, \\mathbf{b} \\, | \\cdot \\sin\\theta \\cdot \\mathbf{n},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\theta$ is the angle formed between the vectors when their tails are placed at the same point, and\n\n- $\\mathbf{n}$ is the unit vector perpendicular to *both* $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ in the direction determined by the right-hand rule. When the vectors are parallel, their cross product is $\\mathbf0$.\n\nThe cross product satisfies a few properties that we would normally expect multiplication to satisfy. \n\nSpecifically, given the vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and any scalar $\\lambda,$ the following properties hold.\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition and subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{b})\\times \\mathbf{c} & =\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}\\pm \\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c} \\\\ \\mathbf{c}\\times (\\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{b}) & =\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda \\mathbf{a})\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\lambda (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}) \\\\ \\mathbf{a}\\times (\\lambda \\mathbf{b}) & =\\lambda (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see some examples of how we can use these properties."]}
